            "Historical and policy analysis should distinguish the text of an agreement from the rules that govern its interpretation and performance. The 1969 Vienna Convention on the Law of Treaties provides an important framework for treaties between states that fall within its scope. Its provisions on good-faith performance and interpretation are widely taught, but a reviewer must still establish which rules apply to the parties and instrument at issue. A training chapter should not turn a general summary into advice about a particular state's treaty obligations.",
            "Article 26 expresses the principle pacta sunt servanda: a treaty in force is binding upon its parties and must be performed by them in good faith. This principle does not mean that every political announcement is a treaty, that every disputed action is a breach, or that a party can unilaterally decide the legal result. The instrument's status, entry into force, parties, scope, and applicable rules must first be established.",
            "Article 31 sets out a general rule of interpretation that reads treaty terms in good faith according to their ordinary meaning in context and in light of the treaty's object and purpose. Context includes the text, preamble, and annexes, and may include certain related agreements or instruments made in connection with the treaty. Subsequent agreement and practice may also be relevant under the Convention's conditions. An interpreter should use the rule as a structured method, not select only the phrase that best supports a negotiating position.",
            "Article 32 permits recourse to supplementary means, including preparatory work and the circumstances of a treaty's conclusion, to confirm meaning or address outcomes described in the provision. Negotiating history can be valuable, but a single delegate's statement is not automatically the shared intent of all parties. The status, completeness, and context of the record matter. The chapter's source ledger can help explain why a particular document is persuasive or limited.",
            "Interpretation must also account for the instrument's structure. Definitions, exceptions, schedules, annexes, and dispute clauses may qualify a general promise. A provision that appears absolute in isolation may be limited by another article; a broad purpose clause does not necessarily grant an institution power that the operative text withholds. Reading the whole instrument prevents a policy slogan from being mistaken for a legal rule.",
            "The Convention addresses reservations, entry into force, amendment, termination, suspension, and other matters, but those rules involve conditions and exceptions. For example, the possibility of terminating or suspending a treaty for material breach is governed by legal provisions and procedural requirements; it is not a general permission for any party to impose whatever response it considers fair. Analysts should identify the relevant legal basis, applicable procedure, and competent decision-maker before describing a remedy as available.",
