            "The Schuman Declaration proposed a concrete first step: place French and German coal and steel production under a common High Authority within an organization open to other European countries. The declaration connected this sectoral arrangement to peace and economic development, but its proposal was not itself the final treaty. The 1951 Treaty of Paris created the ECSC's legal institutions and specified their powers. Students should distinguish the political declaration from the treaty that implemented it.",
            "The ECSC was sectoral by design. Coal and steel were central to industrial reconstruction and armaments, so shared rules and reporting could make certain production decisions more visible and interdependent. The arrangement did not govern every source of military power, foreign policy, or national security. It operated alongside other institutions, alliances, domestic politics, and economic developments. The claim that pooling these industries made mobilization impossible is too strong; it is more accurate to say that common governance altered information, incentives, and the administrative environment in a strategically important sector.",
