            "Negative evidence also needs care. The absence of a recorded protest may mean that no protest occurred, that records were not preserved, that access was restricted, or that dissent was expressed through another channel. Likewise, a single breach does not show that an agreement was wholly ineffective. The researcher should define what evidence would be expected if the argument were true and consider whether the archive or dataset is capable of revealing it.",
            "A strong conclusion calibrates its verbs to the evidence. 'Created,' 'caused,' and 'prevented' make stronger causal claims than 'contributed to,' 'altered incentives,' or 'provided a forum for.' Strong verbs are appropriate when the evidence supports them; caution should not become a blanket refusal to make judgments. The goal is to make the level of certainty visible rather than to bury a conclusion in vague language.",
