            "This method keeps the Chancellery's normative position distinct from its historical argument. The doctrine may favor accountable custody because it values participation, review, and reciprocal benefit. Historical cases can illuminate the potential and limits of those designs, but they do not prove the doctrine's moral premises. A clear chapter can state both: what the record supports about a case and what the Chancellery recommends for future practice.",
